ry and determine an  exposure level much like auto exposure  on a camera so we can do that because we  have this HDR texture pipeline so now  what happens is you can just load this  texture this one image this one HDR  image into hazel and it will  automatically run some computers to  generate a cube map that looks like this  along with a properly filtered map shine  using on one of Unreal Engine 4  algorithms which they released in a  paper and then also generate this  irradiance map all that happens on the  fly in about half a second on my  computer just by dropping in this image  so let's take a look at what that looks  like so here I've just opened hazelnut  which is kind of the editor for hazel  let me just scale up this gun because  the units were using here are meters and  obviously guns are quite small so this  is what we have kind of by default by  loading this gun model with this  animation let me just stop this  animation because it's kind of gonna get  annoying I just had this basic control  here we can stop the animation but about  to start okay so what you see in the  background is the environment map it's  just being rendered as a skybox and we  can actually control which lot or which  MIT level is being rendered by just  using this control here so you can see I  can kind of bring it all the way to  maximum sharpness here which is just the  kind of the first map mm number 0 and  then I can gradually kind of bring that  into well very extreme low maps which is  basically just a solid color as you can  see here this is just controlling the  background and what we see in the  background now you'll see that this is  very overexposed but that's okay because  we can bring down this exposure and you  can see that we get all of that detail  back because it is in fact an HDR map  let's bring that back a little bit  so we have our gun here and you can see  that it has a roughness here of 0.2 so  if I kind of go into here you can see  that it's slightly blurred 